Keep a write history for one registered memory range.

The access counters keep one write stamp per address, so a cell written by one
piece of code and written over by a later one remembers only the later writer.
For Shiny Mushroom that is the one observation the counters cannot make: which
level-object drew a tilemap cell that a later object then drew over. The trace
logger can answer it, but costs ~2x a whole tilemap rebuild, because it
disassembles every executed instruction while it is on.

So the counter machinery learns to keep history for exactly one caller-chosen
range: `SetWriteLogRange(memoryType, offset, length, capacity)` points it at a
range of one (absolute) memory, and every write into that range appends
`(masterClock, address)` to a preallocated buffer that `GetWriteLog` copies
out. The hot-path cost is one enum compare per write outside the range, two
bounds checks inside it. A run that outgrows `capacity` sets an overflow flag
-- reported by `GetWriteLog` -- rather than wrapping, so a truncated answer
can never pass as a whole one. The entries clear wherever the counters clear
(`ResetCounts`, which a savestate load reaches); the registration survives, so
a caller arms once and reads per run.

Range registration is deliberately singular: one range, one consumer, no
locking beyond what the counters themselves have. Reads are meant to happen
while the machine is stopped, exactly like `GetMemoryAccessCounts`.

diff --git a/Core/Debugger/MemoryAccessCounter.cpp b/Core/Debugger/MemoryAccessCounter.cpp
